proposal.text:
{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "post_button_target",
      "description": "The goals include both before-press and after-press targets, but this field was always annotated as post-press, sometimes using frames showing transport or regrasp rather than release. This breaks PREDICTABILITY and SUFFICIENCY. Additionally, a demonstrated target can move during the final ring shuffle, so its release-time location need not be its execution destination. Retain the field name but redefine it as evidence identifying the goal-selected target and, when necessary, tracking that same ring to its final location. At the video cycle, select the last released target placement before the press for a 'right before' goal, or the first released target placement after the press for a 'right after' goal. Record the earliest shown frame of that placement with a visible gap between the cube and the withdrawing hand. If the selected ring subsequently moves, use an ordered list containing that release frame followed by the last stationary frame before its shuffle, every shown shuffle frame, and the first frame establishing its final settled location. Omit a duplicate label and omit shuffle evidence when the selected ring remains stationary. Carry the value unchanged through execution. Examples: a before-press placement can be 'V192'; the blue after-press placement followed by a ring exchange requires ['V512', 'V704', 'V720', 'V736', 'V752', 'V768']. Do not retain the old episode-order label assignments.",
      "value_type": "keyframe"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "post_button_target",
      "description": "The previous instruction unconditionally selected an after-press placement, despite before-press goals, and its supplied labels sometimes showed a held cube. This made the annotation unpredictable and left the requested destination unidentified. During the video cycle, read the goal's temporal relation and locate the downward button press. For 'right before', identify the instructed cube's last released target placement before that press; for 'right after', identify its first released target placement after it. Save the earliest shown frame of the selected placement with a visible gap between the cube and the withdrawing hand. Then inspect the rest of the video: track the selected ring itself, not merely the location where the cube was released. If that ring moves in a later shuffle, save an ordered list consisting of the release frame, the last stationary frame before the shuffle, every shown frame during the shuffle, and the first frame establishing its final settled position. Remove duplicate labels. If the selected ring does not move, save only the release label. For example, a stationary before-press target may be 'V192', whereas a shuffled after-press target may require ['V512', 'V704', 'V720', 'V736', 'V752', 'V768']. All these observations are available in the single video cycle. Copy the incoming value unchanged at every act cycle."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "The previous rules treated every goal as after-press and assumed a stationary ring layout. Consequently, held cubes were transported toward the wrong demonstrated placement, and a blue target that changed position during a late shuffle was mistaken for its old location. These are SUFFICIENCY failures. Replace the unconditional after-press interpretation with the goal's actual before/after relation, as represented by post_button_target. Remove the stationary-layout assumption: identify the ring under the released cube in the first saved frame and, when the memory is a list, follow that same ring through the saved shuffle frames to its final position. Match its final position to the current scene using the button and final ring layout. Do not press the button in execution. Keep the existing approach, grasp, lift, transport, release, and retreat rules, applying them to this correctly identified destination. In particular, if the held cube is already above that destination within the horizontal tolerance, descend to z=0.005 and open rather than transporting elsewhere. A separate pickup failure came from estimating the cube center and yaw directly from its image silhouette. Strengthen the localization rule: use the center of the cube's top face, preferably in the calibrated current wrist image, and intersect its ray with the approximately z=0.04 top-face plane. Transform two points along a top-face edge into world coordinates before deriving the grasp yaw; image-plane edge angles are not world yaw. Use the resulting horizontal center for the z=0.15 approach and z=0.02 grasp, allowing the stated approach endpoint tolerance. No additional execution-history field is needed.",
  "refused": "Do not change the release criterion to transport or placement-contact merely to reproduce the old labels. Those labels contradicted the stated release-and-separation criterion; correcting the annotation is preferable to making a held cube count as released. Also reject the claim that the blue cube was first released after the press on the front-image lower-right ring. The demonstration shows that release on the lower-left ring, followed later by a ring shuffle that takes the selected ring to the lower-right execution destination. A release keyframe alone plus stationary-layout matching would therefore still fail. Preserve the ring's identity through that shuffle rather than treating the recorded execution destination as evidence of where the demonstrated release occurred."
}

